# Claude Code Tooling in the chezmoi Dotfiles Repo

This machine's Claude Code skills, MCP servers, plugins, settings, and global preferences are
managed by the chezmoi dotfiles repo at `{{ $repoRoot }}` (source tree at `{{ $sourceDir }}`) —
regardless of which project you're currently in, that's where an edit belongs. Global
preferences are the rule `rules/global-preferences.md`; when one needs to change, edit
`{{ $sourceDir }}/dot_claude/rules/global-preferences.md.tmpl`, not any profile's `CLAUDE.md`.
This machine may run multiple named Claude Code profiles besides the unnamed default, each with
its own `CLAUDE_CONFIG_DIR` — resolve it explicitly as `${CLAUDE_CONFIG_DIR:-$HOME/.claude}`
rather than assuming a bare `~/.claude/` path.

## Skills, MCP Servers, and Plugins

- **Native skills** (`{{ $sourceDir }}/dot_claude/skills/`) are authored in this repo; `ls`
  there is authoritative over any hand-maintained list.
- **External skills, MCP servers, and plugins/marketplaces** are declared under
  `packages.darwin.ai.agents.claude_code` in `{{ $sourceDir }}/.chezmoidata/packages.yaml` and
  installed from there by chezmoi scripts — that file is the source of truth, not anything
  found under `~/.claude/skills/` on disk (native and external skills land in the same
  deployed directory, so it's a merge of both).
- The `{{ $localMarketplaceName }}` plugin marketplace (`dot_local/share/claude-plugins/`) is a
  deliberate exception: declaring it only makes its plugins installable — enabling one for a
  given project is still its own `/plugin install` there, not a machine-wide default.
- **Removing or disabling a declared item**: edit `packages.yaml`, don't add a local
  `skillOverrides`/`disabledMcpServers` entry — that only drifts from the declared state, it
  doesn't undo it.

## Profiles

`skills/` and `rules/` are shared via symlink across every profile — edit the source under
`{{ $sourceDir }}/dot_claude/`, never a deployed copy under `~/.claude/`; the change reaches
every profile after one `chezmoi apply`. What's **not** shared: {{ $notSharedLabeled | join ", " }}.
Each resolves as `${CLAUDE_CONFIG_DIR:-$HOME/.claude}/<name>`, except `.claude.json`, which is
`$CLAUDE_CONFIG_DIR/.claude.json` when set and `~/.claude.json` (in the home directory, not in
`~/.claude/`) when unset. A profile's managed baseline for `settings.json` lives at
`{{ $sourceDir }}/dot_claude*/.claude-settings.json`, next to its `modify_settings.json.tmpl` —
edit that, then `chezmoi apply`. Never `chezmoi add`/`re-add` a profile `settings.json`: `add`
replaces its merge script with a snapshot of the live file, and `re-add` does nothing. When an
audit, health check, or cleanup could mean every profile rather than just the active one, ask
which is wanted before running.

## Checking for Override Drift

A profile's live `settings.json` can carry `skillOverrides`/`enabledPlugins` entries, or
changed `permissions`/`env` values, that its managed baseline above doesn't explain — an ad hoc
`/skill`/`/plugin`/`/config` toggle the next `chezmoi apply` will otherwise silently revert or
reassert. Run `check-claude-overrides` to detect these rather than comparing files by hand. To
keep a flagged entry, `check-claude-overrides --fix <profile>
<skillOverrides|enabledPlugins|permissions|env> <key>` writes it into that profile's
`.claude-settings.json`. To drop it instead, that stays manual — the equivalent `/skill`/
`/plugin` command, or just letting the next `chezmoi apply` revert a `permissions`/`env` value.
